# gate-matrix golden — RECORDS CURRENT BEHAVIOUR, NOT DESIRED BEHAVIOUR.
#
# Several rows deliberately freeze KNOWN DEFECTS so that fixing them shows up
# as an intended diff rather than passing unnoticed. Each is listed with the
# delta a correct fix SHOULD produce, so a reviewer can tell an expected
# change from a regression without re-deriving it:
#
#   gate11-open-questions   PARTLY FIXED (A-3) — the stop now names the brief
#                           and quotes the bullet that caused it. state=N is
#                           UNCHANGED and still the defect: a genuine question
#                           deletes the state, so the user cannot resume after
#                           answering it. Terminal cleanup is deliberately KEPT
#                           in A; resumability needs the Release C resume path.
#                           Do not expect state=Y here.
#   gate11n1-none-bullet    FIXED (A-3) — a placeholder bullet no longer stops
#                           the loop. The old note here claimed 77% of briefs;
#                           that figure did not reproduce and is dropped rather
#                           than restated. The number that matters is stops
#                           REMOVED, measured at change time over every
#                           iter-NNN.md brief on the machine: 6 removed, all
#                           six verified by eye as '(none)' placeholders, and
#                           0 real questions newly skipped RELATIVE TO THE
#                           PREVIOUS REGEX. That delta is the whole claim. The
#                           old regex also demanded a bare heading and a
#                           -/*/+ bullet, so sections with numbered or prose
#                           bodies were skipped by BOTH versions and do not
#                           show up in the comparison at all. Dual review
#                           found one such brief written FOR THIS REPO whose
#                           two blocking decisions the detector still walks
#                           past. Do not read this line as coverage.
#   gate11n2-h3-heading     FIXED (A-3) — '###' headings are read, and a
#                           sub-heading nested inside the section no longer
#                           closes it.
#   gate11n3-star-bullet    FIXED (A-3) — '*' and '+' are list markers too.
#                           Indented bullets are deliberately NOT matched: a
#                           note nested under a placeholder would re-stop.
#                           TWO decisions here are load-bearing and measured.
#                           (1) The trailing anchor on the heading STAYS.
#                           Removing it starts stopping on '(unscored)' and
#                           '→ 해소됨' headings — reviewers using the section
#                           for their own notes. That collision is semantic.
#                           (2) An h1 does NOT close the section, matching the
#                           rule replaced. Treating a stray '# note' as a
#                           boundary skips the question under it; over-stopping
#                           is the safe side. Fenced blocks are skipped
#                           whole, so a '#' inside one is quoted material
#                           rather than a boundary — measured as a skipped
#                           question before the fence rule existed.
#                           tests/gate11-openq.test.sh pins all of it.
#   gate01-bad-json         PARTLY FIXED (A-1) — fail_open now explains
#                           itself instead of ending the turn in silence.
#                           Deleting a merely CORRUPT file (killed editor
#                           save, disk-full) is STILL the defect: it discards
#                           a recoverable iteration count and baseline SHA.
#                           Note this row moves via fail_open, which has 13
#                           call sites — not via cleanup_and_approve.
#   gate07-inflight-open-question / -ambiguous-heading  the combination that
#                           had no row and is the most likely real stop. The
#                           prompt says STOP on Open Questions at step 5, the
#                           commit is step 8 and clearing the marker step 9 —
#                           so an OBEDIENT model lands exactly here. Gate 7
#                           sits ahead of Gate 11 and used to answer for it,
#                           naming plan mode and advising the user to let the
#                           iteration finish, i.e. to continue past a
#                           disagreement. Both rows must name the disagreement.
#                           If either reverts to the plan-mode wording, the
#                           gate has stopped consulting the brief.
#   gate11-ambiguous-heading  the third classifier state. state=Y is the whole
#                           point: an Open-Questions-shaped heading the
#                           detector cannot act on PAUSES with state preserved.
#                           It must not terminate (that was a measured
#                           regression on reviewers own notes) and must not
#                           advance (that is the silence the gate exists to
#                           prevent). state=N here means someone made it
#                           terminal; no row at all means it went back to
#                           advancing.
#   NOT IN THIS FILE        The ERR-trap exits have no rows and cannot get
#                           one: observe() drives the hook THROUGH gates, and
#                           a trap fires BETWEEN them. A state file that is
#                           valid JSON but not an object, malformed hook
#                           stdin, and an unwritable .claude all used to exit
#                           silently there — and the trap also deleted the
#                           in-flight marker, so the next fire read the
#                           unfinished iteration as complete. Those live in
#                           tests/silent-exit.test.sh. If you are counting
#                           exit paths, count that file too.
#   gate03-inactive         FIXED (A-1) — carries a message. It was the
#                           silent-deletion path a pause setting
#                           active=false would land on; it now explains
#                           itself if that ever happens.
#   gate10b-max-minutes     PARTLY FIXED (A-1) — the cap now names itself
#                           and says how to continue. state=N is UNCHANGED
#                           and still the defect: the cap DELETES state
#                           rather than pausing it. That second diff belongs
#                           to Release C, not here.
#   gate10-max-iterations   PARTLY FIXED (A-1) — same class as gate10b, same
#                           half done. Expect the deletion diff at C.
#   gate06-idle-timeout     PARTLY FIXED (A-1) — the row now carries a full
#                           message naming the 24h idleness, so the collection
#                           is no longer silent. state=N is UNCHANGED and is
#                           still the frozen half: the timeout DELETES rather
#                           than pauses. Same class as gate10/10b; the deletion
#                           diff belongs to Release C.
#                           (This note itself was stale for one release — it
#                           still claimed an EMPTY message after the row had
#                           gained one. Nothing catches that: the comparison
#                           strips ^# lines, so these annotations are
#                           unverifiable by construction. Read them as prose
#                           that needs reviewing like code, not as assertions.)
#   gate08-plan-relative    PARTLY FIXED (A-1) — carries a message now, via
#                           fail_open. A recoverable CONFIGURATION error
#                           still DESTROYS state, same class as gate01. (A
#                           plan path that is absolute but missing takes the
#                           same branch and has no row at all.)
#   gate06b-stale-crosssession  FIXED (B-1) — no longer a frozen defect.
#                           A state both past the idle timeout AND owned by a
#                           vanished session used to be PRESERVED, because
#                           Gate 4 pauses on session mismatch and the GC sat
#                           behind it: never collected, paused again on every
#                           fire, 214 fires / 58% of the field baseline. Now
#                           pins the repaired behaviour: state=N, log = idle
#                           timeout. Back to state=Y and the GC has fallen
#                           behind the defensive gates again.
#   gate06c/06d-marker-inside/outside-lease  the lease boundary, +/-1h,
#                           derived from MARKER_LEASE_SECONDS. They must
#                           always DISAGREE: 6c state=Y, 6d state=N. If they
#                           ever read the same the exemption has stopped
#                           being bounded, which is defect B in a new form.
#                           Their predecessors set only last_injected_at
#                           apart and did NOT straddle anything: a lease of
#                           10 years left the whole suite green. Measured.
#   gate06e-stale-same-session  state=N. RECORDS A BEHAVIOUR CHANGE, not a
#                           defect. Before the GC moved ahead of the
#                           defensive gates this was a Gate 5 soft-pause that
#                           PRESERVED state. Deliberate — 24h with no
#                           iteration is idle — but see the gate05 note
#                           below, which warns against disarming
#                           same-session pauses. Deletion is stronger than
#                           disarming, so the two notes must be read together.
#   gate06f-marker-future-dated  state=N. A marker timestamped in the FUTURE
#                           earns no exemption. A one-sided age<lease test
#                           passes for every negative age and grants a
#                           permanent exemption; clock skew alone reaches it.
#   gate06d-stale-marker-expired-lease  now AND after a fix: state=N.
#                           Deliberately unchanged. It is the other side of
#                           6c: once the lease expires the marker stops
#                           excusing the state. If 6c and 6d ever agree, the
#                           exemption has stopped being bounded and defect B
#                           is back in a new form.
#   gate09b-dirty-blocks    PARTLY FIXED (B-3) — the pause now carries a
#                           systemMessage naming the gitignore patterns, so
#                           it is no longer silent. Completion is still
#                           unreachable while an untracked state file or
#                           brief keeps the tree dirty: that is the correct
#                           refusal, and the guidance in skills/ + README
#                           is what stops a compliant repo from hitting it.
#   gate09c-plan-outside-repo  FIXED (B-3) — no longer a frozen defect.
#                           It used to declare 'all tasks complete' and
#                           delete state over a DIRTY repo, because Gate 9
#                           checked dirname(plan_path) instead of REPO_ROOT
#                           and a plan at the /plan default is outside any
#                           repo, skipping the check entirely. Now pins the
#                           repaired behaviour: SOFT-PAUSE, state=Y
#                           active=true. Reverting to state=N here means
#                           the repo-scope fix regressed.
#   gate04-session-mismatch PARTLY FIXED (A-1) — the pause now names the
#                           other session and how to resume. This was the
#                           field baseline's single most frequent event
#                           (214 fires / 58%) and it said nothing.
#                           active=true is UNCHANGED. The prescribed fix
#                           (active=false) is DEFERRED to Release C with the
#                           resume path, not withdrawn — disarming without a
#                           way back is a worse trade than the noise.
#   gate05-no-continuation  FIXED (A-1) — message added, active stays true,
#                           which is exactly what this row asked for. Do NOT
#                           fold it in with gate04: disarming a SAME-session
#                           pause would kill a loop its owner still wants.
#                           gate05b-grace-outside moves with it — same call
#                           site, and it is easy to leave out of a count.
#   gate12-lock-contention  lock=Y — a non-owner must never rmdir. If this
#                           returns to lock=N, ownership tracking regressed.
#   gate12-lock-orphan-reclaimed  decision=block — the loop ADVANCES. A lock
#                           with no possible live holder is reclaimed rather
#                           than pausing forever. This pair replaces a single
#                           row that conflated the two: it pre-created a bare
#                           lock dir, labelled it contention, and pinned
#                           soft-pause-forever as correct. Because this gate
#                           precedes EVERY other gate including the idle GC,
#                           that made one orphaned lock wedge the loop
#                           permanently — state could never be collected —
#                           and the only row covering stale locks certified
#                           it as intended. If this row becomes approve/
#                           state=Y, the deadlock is back.
#                           NOTE, measured: simply deleting the rmdir from
#                           soft_pause() flips SIX rows — gate02, gate04,
#                           gate05, gate05b, gate09b, gate12 — because five
#                           of them legitimately acquire the lock and would
#                           then leak it. Those five reading lock=N is what
#                           proves owner paths still release rather than leak.
#   gate13-brief-path-collision  the brief number is derived from a
#                           hand-seeded counter, so it can be rewound and name
#                           a file that already holds the previous iteration's
#                           evidence. The injected prompt says to write there
#                           verbatim, so a collision is silent data loss. Pins
#                           the skip to the first free name: if the log tail
#                           names iter-001 again, the overwrite is back.
#                           Not covered here: the exhausted-window refusal
#                           (soft_pause, state preserved) and the >999
#                           boundary — both need a directory the matrix does
#                           not build, and are driven directly instead.
#
# Do not read this file as a specification. A diff here is a question
# ("did I mean to change this?"), not automatically a failure. Regenerating
# with --update is how you ACCEPT a delta — walk the rows above first.
#
# Columns (6, pipe-separated):
#   1 label  2 decision  3 state/active/marker(value)/lock/iter
#   4 reason fingerprint (sentinel line + enforcement-clause count)
#   5 systemMessage  6 log tail
# Regenerate: bash tests/gate-matrix.test.sh --update
advance-inject             | block   | state=Y active=true  marker=Y(2) lock=N iter=2/2 | sentinel="[dual-review-loop iter 2/20]" enforce=1 | dual-review-loop plan iter 2/20 | iter 2 → injecting (brief target: <TMP>/.claude/reviews/iter-002.md)
gate00-no-state            | approve | state=N active=-     marker=N(-) lock=N iter=-/- | - |  | 
gate01-bad-json            | approve | state=N active=-     marker=N(-) lock=N iter=-/- | - | dual-review-loop stopped on an error it cannot recover from (state file is not a JSON object (unparseable, or parses to an array/string/number)). Its state file and marker were cleared; nothing you committed was touched. Fix the cause and start a new loop. | FAIL-OPEN: state file is not a JSON object (unparseable, or parses to an array/string/number)
gate02-schema-unknown      | approve | state=Y active=true  marker=N(-) lock=N iter=1/0 | - | dual-review-loop paused: state schema 'v9' unknown. To resume: install a hook supporting this schema, OR run /drl-cancel (or rm <TMP>/.claude/dual-review-loop.state.json) to start over. | SOFT-PAUSE: schema mismatch (got=v9 expected one of: v1 v2) — state preserved; downgrade hook or cancel manually (state preserved)
gate03-inactive            | approve | state=N active=-     marker=N(-) lock=N iter=-/- | - | dual-review-loop: the loop was already marked inactive, so its state was cleared. Nothing you committed was touched. | state.active != true
gate04-session-mismatch    | approve | state=Y active=true  marker=N(-) lock=N iter=1/0 | - | dual-review-loop: this loop belongs to a different Claude Code session, so this turn did not advance it. Its state is untouched. Resume it from the session that started it, or run /drl-cancel to clear it. | SOFT-PAUSE: different session (test-session != other-session) (state preserved)
gate05-no-continuation     | approve | state=Y active=true  marker=N(-) lock=N iter=1/1 | - | dual-review-loop: the loop did not advance because this turn does not look like a continuation of iteration 1 — you likely took over manually. The state is preserved; it picks up again on a turn that follows its instructions, or run /drl-cancel to stop it. | SOFT-PAUSE: no continuation signal (last_injected_iter=1, gap=<N>s) — user likely took control (state preserved)
gate05a-grace-inside       | block   | state=Y active=true  marker=Y(2) lock=N iter=2/2 | sentinel="[dual-review-loop iter 2/20]" enforce=1 | dual-review-loop plan iter 2/20 | iter 2 → injecting (brief target: <TMP>/.claude/reviews/iter-002.md)
gate05b-grace-outside      | approve | state=Y active=true  marker=N(-) lock=N iter=1/1 | - | dual-review-loop: the loop did not advance because this turn does not look like a continuation of iteration 1 — you likely took over manually. The state is preserved; it picks up again on a turn that follows its instructions, or run /drl-cancel to stop it. | SOFT-PAUSE: no continuation signal (last_injected_iter=1, gap=<N>s) — user likely took control (state preserved)
gate06-idle-timeout        | approve | state=N active=-     marker=N(-) lock=N iter=-/- | - | dual-review-loop: this loop had no activity for over 24h, so its state was collected and the loop has ended. Nothing you committed was touched — only the plugin's own state file and marker. To pick the work back up, start a new loop on the same plan. | idle timeout (>86400s)
gate06-idle-while-held     | approve | state=N active=-     marker=N(-) lock=N iter=-/- | - | dual-review-loop ended after more than 24h. It was NOT idle — it was holding for a decision in the review brief that was never answered: A says drop the index, B says keep it. Brief: <TMP>/.claude/reviews/iter-001.md. Its state was cleared; answer the question and start a new loop. | idle timeout (>86400s) while held on an open question
gate06-idle-while-held-ambiguous | approve | state=N active=-     marker=N(-) lock=N iter=-/- | - | dual-review-loop ended after more than 24h. It was NOT idle — it was holding on a section shaped like an open question that it could not read as a decision, and nobody resolved it: ## Open Questions → 해소됨. Brief: <TMP>/.claude/reviews/iter-001.md. Check whether that section was a decision for you. Its state was cleared; start a new loop when you know. | idle timeout (>86400s) while held on an unreadable Open Questions section
gate06b-stale-crosssession | approve | state=N active=-     marker=N(-) lock=N iter=-/- | - | dual-review-loop: this loop had no activity for over 24h, so its state was collected and the loop has ended. Nothing you committed was touched — only the plugin's own state file and marker. To pick the work back up, start a new loop on the same plan. | idle timeout (>86400s)
gate06c-marker-inside-lease | approve | state=Y active=true  marker=Y(1) lock=N iter=1/0 | - | dual-review-loop: iter 1 has not committed yet (plan mode can block commits, or it stopped early) and its brief shows no open question. It auto-resumes the moment a commit lands — exit plan mode and let it finish. If this iteration legitimately produced no commit, run /drl-cancel (or rm .claude/dual-review-loop.inflight). | in-flight marker present (iter=1) — no commit detected; not advancing (base_sha=<SHA>)
gate06d-marker-outside-lease | approve | state=N active=-     marker=N(-) lock=N iter=-/- | - | dual-review-loop: this loop had no activity for over 24h, so its state was collected and the loop has ended. Nothing you committed was touched — only the plugin's own state file and marker. To pick the work back up, start a new loop on the same plan. | idle timeout (>86400s)
gate06e-stale-same-session | approve | state=N active=-     marker=N(-) lock=N iter=-/- | - | dual-review-loop: this loop had no activity for over 24h, so its state was collected and the loop has ended. Nothing you committed was touched — only the plugin's own state file and marker. To pick the work back up, start a new loop on the same plan. | idle timeout (>86400s)
gate06f-marker-future-dated | approve | state=N active=-     marker=N(-) lock=N iter=-/- | - | dual-review-loop: this loop had no activity for over 24h, so its state was collected and the loop has ended. Nothing you committed was touched — only the plugin's own state file and marker. To pick the work back up, start a new loop on the same plan. | idle timeout (>86400s)
gate07-inflight-ambiguous-heading | approve | state=Y active=true  marker=Y(1) lock=N iter=1/0 | - | dual-review-loop: iter 1 did not commit, and its brief has a heading shaped like an open question that the loop cannot read as a decision — ## Open Questions (unscored). Brief: <TMP>/.claude/reviews/iter-001.md. Check whether that section is a decision for you. If it is not, rename the heading to anything that does not begin with 'Open Questions' and the loop continues; /drl-cancel stops it instead. | in-flight marker present (iter=1) — no commit detected; not advancing (base_sha=<SHA>)
gate07-inflight-nocommit   | approve | state=Y active=true  marker=Y(1) lock=N iter=1/0 | - | dual-review-loop: iter 1 has not committed yet (plan mode can block commits, or it stopped early) and its brief shows no open question. It auto-resumes the moment a commit lands — exit plan mode and let it finish. If this iteration legitimately produced no commit, run /drl-cancel (or rm .claude/dual-review-loop.inflight). | in-flight marker present (iter=1) — no commit detected; not advancing (base_sha=<SHA>)
gate07-inflight-open-question | approve | state=Y active=true  marker=Y(1) lock=N iter=1/0 | - | dual-review-loop: iter 1 stopped on a reviewer disagreement, not on a commit problem. The brief has an open question and the loop must not decide it for you — A says drop the index, B says keep it. Brief: <TMP>/.claude/reviews/iter-001.md. Answer it, then run /drl-cancel and start a new loop. Do NOT just clear the marker to make this go away: that lets the loop continue past a disagreement nobody settled. | in-flight marker present (iter=1) — no commit detected; not advancing (base_sha=<SHA>)
gate08-plan-missing-open-question | approve | state=N active=-     marker=N(-) lock=N iter=-/- | - | dual-review-loop stopped on an error it cannot recover from (plan_path missing: <TMP>/gone.md). Its state file and marker were cleared; nothing you committed was touched. Fix the cause and start a new loop. NOTE: the last review brief still holds an unanswered question — A says drop the index, B says keep it (brief: <TMP>/.claude/reviews/iter-001.md). Ending here does not resolve it. | FAIL-OPEN: plan_path missing: <TMP>/gone.md
gate08-plan-relative       | approve | state=N active=-     marker=N(-) lock=N iter=-/- | - | dual-review-loop stopped on an error it cannot recover from (plan_path not absolute: relative/plan.md). Its state file and marker were cleared; nothing you committed was touched. Fix the cause and start a new loop. | FAIL-OPEN: plan_path not absolute: relative/plan.md
gate09-complete-open-question | approve | state=N active=-     marker=N(-) lock=N iter=-/- | - | dual-review-loop: stopped because the review brief has a question that needs your decision. Brief: <TMP>/.claude/reviews/iter-001.md. First item: A says drop the index, B says keep it. Note this section means the two REVIEWERS DISAGREED — a reviewer's own follow-up notes belong under a different heading. Answer it, then start a new loop — note that a new loop re-baselines the cumulative caps (max_files/max_loc/max_reviews) to the current HEAD, so in task mode the budget starts over. | Open Questions detected in last brief — user decision needed
gate09a-all-complete       | approve | state=N active=-     marker=N(-) lock=N iter=-/- | - | dual-review-loop: every task in the plan is checked off — the loop finished after 1 iteration(s). The review briefs are in .claude/reviews/. | all tasks complete after 1 iterations
gate09b-dirty-blocks       | approve | state=Y active=true  marker=N(-) lock=N iter=1/0 | - | dual-review-loop: every task in the plan is complete, but the working tree is not clean, so the loop did not declare completion. 1 path(s), starting with: ?? .claude/. Commit or stash, or ignore it, and the loop finishes on the next turn. Note this counts UNTRACKED files too, including output from tools that have nothing to do with this loop — an untracked .omc/ has stalled a real run. If it is this plugin's own output, the patterns are .claude/dual-review-loop.*, .claude/dual-review-loop/ and .claude/reviews/. | SOFT-PAUSE: no unfinished tasks but uncommitted changes present (state preserved)
gate09c-plan-outside-repo  | approve | state=Y active=true  marker=N(-) lock=N iter=1/0 | - | dual-review-loop: every task in the plan is complete, but the working tree is not clean, so the loop did not declare completion. 1 path(s), starting with: M src.txt. Commit or stash, or ignore it, and the loop finishes on the next turn. Note this counts UNTRACKED files too, including output from tools that have nothing to do with this loop — an untracked .omc/ has stalled a real run. If it is this plugin's own output, the patterns are .claude/dual-review-loop.*, .claude/dual-review-loop/ and .claude/reviews/. | SOFT-PAUSE: no unfinished tasks but uncommitted changes present (state preserved)
gate10-max-iterations      | approve | state=N active=-     marker=N(-) lock=N iter=-/- | - | dual-review-loop: stopped at the iteration cap (20 of 20), with tasks still unfinished in the plan. Start a new loop with a higher --max-iters to continue. | max_iterations reached (20 >= 20)
gate10-maxiter-ambiguous   | approve | state=N active=-     marker=N(-) lock=N iter=-/- | - | dual-review-loop: stopped at the iteration cap (20 of 20), with tasks still unfinished in the plan. Start a new loop with a higher --max-iters to continue. NOTE: the last review brief has a section shaped like an open question that the loop could not read — ## Open Questions (unscored) (brief: <TMP>/.claude/reviews/iter-001.md). Worth a look before you treat this run as settled. | max_iterations reached (20 >= 20)
gate10-maxiter-open-question | approve | state=N active=-     marker=N(-) lock=N iter=-/- | - | dual-review-loop: stopped at the iteration cap (20 of 20), with tasks still unfinished in the plan. Start a new loop with a higher --max-iters to continue. NOTE: the last review brief still holds an unanswered question — A says drop the index, B says keep it (brief: <TMP>/.claude/reviews/iter-001.md). Ending here does not resolve it. | max_iterations reached (20 >= 20)
gate10b-max-minutes        | approve | state=N active=-     marker=N(-) lock=N iter=-/- | - | dual-review-loop: stopped at the wall-clock cap (1 min). This measures elapsed time, not work done — the clock runs while the loop waits for you. Start a new loop to continue. | max_minutes reached (<N>s >= 60s / 1min cap)
gate11-ambiguous-heading   | approve | state=Y active=true  marker=N(-) lock=N iter=1/0 | - | dual-review-loop paused: the brief has a heading shaped like an Open Questions section that the loop cannot read as a decision — ## Open Questions (진짜 결정 필요). The loop holds rather than guess, because this section is how a reviewer disagreement reaches you. Two ways out, and they do NOT do the same thing. (1) It is only a reviewer's note: rename the heading to anything not beginning with 'Open Questions' and the loop RESUMES where it left off. (2) It is a real decision: rename it to exactly '## Open Questions' with each item on its own top-level bullet — the loop then ENDS and hands it to you, clearing its state (in task mode that also re-baselines the file/LOC/review budgets). Until you do one of those this message repeats every turn, and after 24h the loop is collected. Brief: <TMP>/.claude/reviews/iter-001.md. /drl-cancel stops it now. | SOFT-PAUSE: ambiguous Open Questions in brief (## Open Questions (진짜 결정 필요)) (state preserved)
gate11-open-questions      | approve | state=N active=-     marker=N(-) lock=N iter=-/- | - | dual-review-loop: stopped because the review brief has a question that needs your decision. Brief: <TMP>/.claude/reviews/iter-001.md. First item: a real question. Note this section means the two REVIEWERS DISAGREED — a reviewer's own follow-up notes belong under a different heading. Answer it, then start a new loop — note that a new loop re-baselines the cumulative caps (max_files/max_loc/max_reviews) to the current HEAD, so in task mode the budget starts over. | Open Questions detected in last brief — user decision needed
